<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Latvian Self-Administration</h1><p class="st-meta">1942 CE – 1945 CE · capital Riga</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Latvian auxiliary administration under German occupation from 1942 to 1945, headed by General Oskars Dankers.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> placed occupied <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-latvia">Latvia</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/generalkommissariat-lettland">Generalkommissariat Lettland</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/reichskommissariat-ostland">Reichskommissariat Ostland</a>. Latvian directors-general were appointed in 1941 to handle local affairs, and in March 1942 the structure was formalised as the Latvian Self-Administration under General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oskars-dankers">Oskars Dankers</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Self-Administration had no political independence and executed German orders on economic, educational and social matters. In 1943 the Germans used it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conscription">conscript</a> Latvian men into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvian-legion">Latvian Legion</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waffen-ss">Waffen-SS</a>. Most of Latvia's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> had already been murdered in 1941, notably at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rumbula-massacre">Rumbula</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> retook Riga in October 1944, and the administration moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, ending with the German surrender in May 1945. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-latvia">Latvia</a> was again governed as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/latvian-soviet-socialist-republic">Latvian Soviet Socialist Republic</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="other, 1942 CE – 1945 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M26 24 H74 V52 Q74 74 50 84 Q26 74 26 52 Z"/><path d="M50 24 V84 M26 46 H74"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">other</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:98.35%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1942 CE – 1945 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Riga</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Oskars Dankers</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheran and Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latvian, German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/latvia">Latvia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Latvijas Zemes pašpārvalde, Latvian Directorate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1944&amp;focus=latvian-self-administration" aria-label="Latvian Self-Administration on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5200 245 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Latvian Self-Administration lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5520 501L5520 501"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5520 501h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5520 501h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5520 501h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Riga. <a href="/atlas?year=1944&amp;focus=latvian-self-administration">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

